{"title":"Glossary","markdown":{"headingText":"Glossary","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-glossary","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nThese definitions use the meanings applied in Volume I. Follow the relevant chapter when a quantity has several possible conventions or requires design-specific interpretation.\n\n## Data and design\n\n| Term | Meaning in this book |\n|:-----|:---------------------|\n| Analysis-ready data | A reviewed file with documented coding, eligibility, units, and missing-value rules |\n| Analysis specification | A stated question, target quantity, eligible sample, variables, method, and reporting plan |\n| Binary variable | A variable with two defined categories |\n| Categorical variable | A variable whose values indicate categories rather than a continuous numerical amount |\n| Complete case | An observation with values for all variables required by the specified analysis |\n| Complete pair | Two matched measurements both available for the paired calculation |\n| Continuous variable | A quantitative measurement treated on a numerical scale with meaningful distances |\n| Data dictionary | Definitions of variables, units, categories, timing, missing codes, and derivations |\n| Data type | Storage property such as integer, decimal, or text |\n| Data validation | Checks of whether the file follows its measurement and entry definitions |\n| Derived variable | A new quantity calculated or recoded from documented source variables |\n| Filter | A defined rule controlling which observations enter analyses |\n| Identifier | A label used to identify or link observation units |\n| Independent observations | Observations treated as independent under the design and analysis model; repeated or clustered records need assessment |\n| Long format | Data with measurements across occasions represented in separate rows and linked by identifiers |\n| Measure type | Analytical property such as nominal, ordinal, continuous, or ID |\n| Missing value | An unavailable intended measurement or status; not automatically a zero or negative result |\n| Missing at random (MAR) | Missingness independent of missing values conditional on relevant observed information |\n| Missing completely at random (MCAR) | Missingness unrelated to observed and unobserved values |\n| Missing not at random (MNAR) | Dependence of missingness on unobserved values remains |\n| Nominal variable | Categories with no inherent order |\n| Ordinal variable | Ordered categories without an automatic equal-spacing interpretation |\n| Paired data | Observations related by a defined match, such as two measurements from the same participant |\n| Row key | A variable or combination of variables identifying a row under the defined observation unit |\n| Unit of observation | The entity represented by an analytical record, such as a patient, visit, admission, or facility |\n| Wide format | Data with different measurements represented in separate columns for the same unit |\n\nThe software distinctions between data type, measure type, and derived variables are documented in jamovi. Complete-case status is defined relative to the variables used in a calculation. Missingness assumptions concern the data process. [@jamoviData; @jamoviComputed; @RComplete; @RubinMissing]\n\n## Description and uncertainty\n\n| Term | Meaning in this book |\n|:-----|:---------------------|\n| Confidence interval | An interval produced by a procedure with a stated coverage rate under its assumptions |\n| Denominator | The set or count relative to which a proportion is calculated |\n| Estimate | A sample-based value used to learn about a target parameter |\n| Estimand | The precisely defined quantity the analysis aims to estimate |\n| Frequency | The number of observations in a category or interval |\n| Geometric mean | For positive values, the exponential of the average of their natural logarithms |\n| Interquartile range | Third quartile minus first quartile |\n| Mean | Sum of the observations divided by their number |\n| Median | Middle ordered value, with the two middle values averaged when n is even |\n| Mode | Most frequent value or category; it need not be unique |\n| Parameter | A quantity describing the population or statistical model |\n| Percentage point | A unit for an absolute difference between percentages |\n| Precision | The degree of uncertainty in an estimate under the selected procedure |\n| Proportion | A defined count divided by an appropriate denominator |\n| Quartile | A value dividing an ordered distribution at a specified quarter; interpolation conventions can vary |\n| Range | The span from minimum to maximum, or their difference when explicitly stated |\n| Sampling variability | Variation in a statistic across repeated samples under the sampling model |\n| Standard deviation | Dispersion of observations around their mean, in the original unit |\n| Standard error | Estimated sampling variability of an estimator, distinct from individual spread |\n| Standardisation | Expressing a value relative to a stated reference mean and SD |\n| Statistic | A quantity calculated from the observed sample |\n| Variance | Squared standard deviation under the corresponding definition |\n\nThe location, spread, quantile, and mean-interval definitions are described in the statistical reference documentation. [@NISTLocation; @NISTScale; @RQuantile; @NISTCI]\n\n## Testing and interpretation\n\n| Term | Meaning in this book |\n|:-----|:---------------------|\n| Alternative hypothesis | The specified departure from the null evaluated by a test |\n| Bias | A systematic problem in an estimator or study process relative to its target; not ordinary sampling variation |\n| Clinical importance | Relevance of an effect magnitude to the stated clinical objective, distinct from a p-value threshold |\n| Contrast | A defined comparison, such as A minus B or A relative to B |\n| Degrees of freedom | A parameter of a test's reference distribution, determined by its procedure |\n| Multiplicity | The problem of interpreting several related analyses and their combined error criterion |\n| Null hypothesis | The specified value or relationship evaluated under a test model |\n| Omnibus test | An overall assessment across a set of groups or terms rather than one specific pair |\n| P-value | Probability of the chosen test statistic being at least as incompatible as observed, conditional on the specified null model |\n| Post-hoc comparison | A follow-up comparison whose procedure and multiplicity handling must be identified |\n| Power | Probability of rejecting the null under a specified alternative and test procedure |\n| Reference category | The category against which a contrast or model coefficient is expressed |\n| Significance level | The nominal Type I error control level under the test's assumptions |\n| Type I error | Rejecting a true null under the test procedure |\n| Type II error | Failing to reject the null under a specified true alternative |\n\nInterpretation of p-values, errors, and power depends on the conditioning and model. A non-significant result does not alone establish equivalence. [@ASAp; @NISTTesting; @RPowerT; @BMJAbsence]\n\n## Association and modeling\n\n| Term | Meaning in this book |\n|:-----|:---------------------|\n| Cramér's V | A scaled categorical-association summary based on a chi-square statistic and table dimensions |\n| Expected count | Cell frequency implied by the specified table model, such as independence |\n| Fitted value | The model's estimated response quantity at a specified predictor value |\n| Homoscedasticity | A constant error variance under the relevant model |\n| Influence | The extent to which an observation affects the fitted result |\n| Intercept | The model quantity at the predictor's reference or zero value |\n| Leverage | A property related to an observation's predictor position relative to the fitted design |\n| Linear association | A relationship summarised by a straight-line pattern |\n| Logit | Natural logarithm of probability divided by its complement |\n| Monotonic association | An ordered relationship that moves in one direction, not necessarily in a straight line |\n| Odds | Probability divided by one minus that probability |\n| Odds ratio | Ratio of two odds; the null value is one |\n| Outcome | The variable whose distribution or relationship is the focus of the analysis |\n| Pearson correlation | A standardised summary of linear association between paired quantitative values |\n| Prediction interval | An interval for a new observation under a model, including individual variation |\n| Predictor | A variable used to specify or explain a model outcome; the role does not establish causation |\n| Residual | Observed value minus fitted value for the relevant model quantity |\n| Risk difference | Difference between design-supported risks, often expressed in percentage points |\n| Risk ratio | Ratio of design-supported risks |\n| R-squared | In ordinary linear regression with an intercept, the share of observed squared outcome variation accounted for by the fit |\n| Separation | A binary-model data pattern that prevents a finite ordinary maximum-likelihood estimate in the affected direction |\n| Slope | Change in the modeled outcome quantity for a defined predictor increment |\n| Spearman correlation | Correlation of ranks used to summarise monotonic association |\n| Unadjusted model | A model that does not control for additional characteristics beyond its stated predictor structure |\n\nThe categorical, correlation, linear, and generalised-linear-model references provide the associated calculations and output definitions. [@RChiSq; @RCor; @RLm; @RSummaryLm; @RGlm; @RPredictLm; @BMJOdds]\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,